      "problem": "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{21}-16{x}^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\,\\,{x}^{19}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">${x}^{2}-16\\,$</td>\n<td colspan=\"7\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{21}\\,-\\,16{x}^{19}\\,+\\,6x\\,\\,-\\,\\,3\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">${x}^{21}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$16{x}^{19}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$6x$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =6A-3I \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
